+def door_end_teed(lanes: Sequence[Mapping[str, Any]], i: int, end: int, alone: bool, other: Pt) -> bool:
+    """Is lane `i`'s `end` a household way's DOOR end that meets no way - an access lane's first end, `alone` at its node and
+    on no other lane's side - with `other` on that same lane, so that the one lane runs between them? Such a pair is a T near
+    a door, not two ends that nearly meet: 0081's knot is ends left loose beside each other and three lanes arriving a few
+    feet apart, and here one drawn lane already joins the two (the reading `knots`' own docstring states: "a 15 ft door lane
+    from the track's head is not two arrivals"). The knot VERDICT reads it (the pool test's `lane_knots`); the gather search
+    (`knots`, `next_gather`) still tries to join such a pair where it lawfully can - exempted there too, the gathers it drops
+    had been untangling real knots beside them, and Kuwabata and Sawada were left knotted (feature 328 wave 92, measured;
+    spec-fidelity's ruling of 2026-10-10: LEGITIMATE in this narrow form; a door end within the reach of another lane's end
+    or foot, or a door end that is itself a junction, is still a knot).
+
+    Research: a door end teed on its own lane - research/questions/0081-village-lanes.drawing.html: lane ends that nearly meet are joined, so three lanes never arrive a few feet apart in a knot"""
+    ln = lanes[i]
+    if end != 0 or not alone or ln.get("role") != ACCESS_ROLE:
+        return False
+    pts = _pts(ln)
+    if len(pts) < 2:
+        return False
+    q = pts[0]
+    if any(k != i and len(_pts(o)) >= 2 and _on(_pts(o), q) for k, o in enumerate(lanes)):
+        return False
+    return _on(pts, other)
+
